<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-section-hypergeometric-DRV" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Hypergeometric Random Variable</title>

<introduction>
 <p> 
    By completing the problems provided below, you will gain experience using the Hypergeometric random variable. Specifically, you will practice: 
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Identifying when the Hypergeometric distribution can be used to model a scenario.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
         Identifying when the Hypergeometric distribution can be approximated by the Binomial distribution. 
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Using the Hypergeometric probability distribution function to compute probabilities.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
            Using the expected value and variance formulas for a Hypergeometric random variable.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
</introduction>


 <exercises xml:id="hw-hyper-rv" >
   <title>Interactive Exercises</title>



<exercise label="ex-is-this-hypergeometric">
  <title>Is this Hypergeometric? </title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Which of the following random variables <m>X</m> can be modeled using a 
     Hypergeometric distribution? Select all that apply.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <choices randomize="yes">
    <!-- CORRECT: Classic finite population without replacement (lot inspection) -->
    <choice correct="yes">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A shipping crate contains <m>30</m> ceramic mugs, of which <m>5</m> have hairline 
          cracks. A quality inspector randomly selects a sample of <m>6</m> mugs from the crate. Let <m>X</m> be the number of cracked mugs in the sample.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          Correct! We are drawing a fixed sample of size <m>n = 6</m> <em>without replacement</em> (implied) 
          from a finite population of <m>N = 30</m> mugs containing <m>A = 5</m> 
          cracked mugs (<q>successes</q>) and <m>B=25</m> ok mugs, and we are counting how many successes appear in the sample.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>

    <!-- CORRECT: Committee/team selection without replacement -->
    <choice correct="yes">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A university department has <m>5</m> statisticians,  <m>3</m> data scientists, <m>9</m> mathematicians, and <m>7</m> computer scientists. 
          A curriculum committee of <m>4</m> faculty members is formed by selecting names at random from a hat. Let <m>X</m> be the number of statisticians 
          chosen for the committee.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          Correct! The population consists of <m>N = 24</m> faculty members, which can be split into two 
          groups (<m>A = 5</m> statisticians and <m>B=19</m> other faculty), and we draw a sample of
          <m>n = 4</m> individuals <em>without replacement</em> (implied) while counting the number from the first group in the sample.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>

    <!-- DISTRACTOR 1: Sampling WITH replacement (Binomial) -->
    <choice correct="no">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A bag contains <m>12</m> red marbles and <m>18</m> blue marbles. A student draws 
          a marble at random, records its color, and places it back into the bag before 
          shaking and drawing again. This is repeated <m>8</m> times. Let <m>X</m> be the 
          number of red marbles drawn.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          Incorrect. Because each marble is returned to the bag before the next draw, the 
          trials are independent with a constant success probability (<m>p = 12/30 = 0.4</m>). 
          This describes a Binomial random variable, rather than a Hypergeometric one.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>

    <!-- DISTRACTOR 2: Waiting for r-th success without replacement (Negative Hypergeometric) -->
    <choice correct="no">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A standard <m>52</m>-card deck is thoroughly shuffled, and cards are dealt one at a 
          time without replacement until the <m>2\text{nd}</m> Heart is revealed. Let 
          <m>X</m> be the total number of cards dealt.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          Incorrect. While this involves sampling without replacement from a finite deck, the 
          sample size is random — we stop upon reaching <m>2</m> successes, rather than 
          taking a fixed sample of size <m>n</m>. 
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>

    <!-- DISTRACTOR 3: Large/infinite population with constant p (Binomial) -->
    <choice correct="no">
      <statement>
        <p>
          At a large automated bottling plant, <m>2\%</m> of all bottles produced are underfilled, 
          independently from bottle to bottle. An auditor pulls <m>25</m> bottles from the 
          continuous production line to test them. Let <m>X</m> be the number of underfilled 
          bottles in the sample.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          Incorrect. The bottles are produced independently from a continuous process with a 
          constant defect rate <m>p = 0.02</m>, rather than being drawn without replacement 
          from a small, fixed finite batch. This follows a Binomial distribution.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>

    <!-- DISTRACTOR 4: More than two categories (Multivariate Hypergeometric) 
    <choice correct="no">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A box holds <m>10</m> dark chocolates, <m>10</m> milk chocolates, and <m>10</m> white 
          chocolates. You grab <m>6</m> chocolates at random without replacement and record the 
          vector <m>(X_1, X_2, X_3)</m> giving the count of each flavor selected.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          Incorrect. A univariate hypergeometric random variable counts successes from a single 
          binary classification ("success" vs. "failure"). Tracking counts across three distinct 
          categories simultaneously requires a <strong>multivariate hypergeometric</strong> distribution.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>-->
  </choices>

   <hint>
        <p>
      To determine if a variable fits the hypergeometric model, check for these three features:
</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Binary - Can the population be divided 
          into two mutually exclusive groups with <m>A</m> items classified as Type I (or "successes") and 
          <m>B</m> items classified as Type II (or "failures"), where the total population size is <m>N=A+B?</m>
        </p>
      </li>
      
      <li>
        <p>
 Fixed sample size - Is a sample with a fixed size, <m>n \le N</m>, drawn from the population?
        </p>
      </li>
<li>
        <p>
      Sampling without replacement - When the sample is drawn, are items selected without replacement, 
          so trials are <em>not</em> independent and the probability of success changes from draw to draw?
        </p>
      </li>

    </ul>
  </hint>

</exercise>


<!--start here-->
<exercise xml:id="ex-cookie-jar-hypergeometric-expanded">
    <title> The Cookie Jar Problem</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            A cookie jar currently holds <m>21</m> total cookies: <m>13</m> are chocolate-chip cookies, and <m>8</m> are brussels-sprout-with-turnip-chip cookies. You pick <m>3</m> cookies at random from the jar without looking. Let the random variable <m>X</m> represent the number of brussels-sprout-with-turnip-chip cookies found in your sample.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- Part 1: Explaining WHY it is Hypergeometric 
    <task xml:id="task-cookie-rv-why-hypergeometric-v2">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let the random variable <m>X</m> represent the number of brussels-sprout-with-turnip-chip cookies found in your sample. This scenario is modeled using a <term>Hypergeometric distribution</term>. Which of the following best explains why the Hypergeometric model applies here?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                    Because we are sampling a fixed number of items from a finite population containing two distinct categories without replacement, meaning individual trial outcomes are dependent.
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        Correct! A hypergeometric random variable tracks a fixed sample size drawn from a small finite population divided into two groups. Because cookies are eaten or set aside (sampling <term>without replacement</term>), the probability of drawing a specific cookie type shifts dynamically with each draw, making the choices dependent.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    Because each draw is independent, and the probability of picking a brussels-sprout-with-turnip-chip cookie remains perfectly constant at <m>\frac{8}{21}</m> for all three selections.
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        Incorrect. Constant probabilities and trial independence only hold true if you put each cookie back into the jar before selecting the next one (sampling with replacement), which fits a Binomial distribution.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    Because we are tracking the infinite number of attempts required to pull our very first edible chocolate-chip cookie.
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        Incorrect. Counting the number of independent trials required until a success occurs describes a Geometric distribution, not a Hypergeometric distribution.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    Because the cookies are randomly distributed over a continuous geometric interval of volume or surface area.
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        Incorrect. Continuous intervals track Poisson patterns or geometric density functions, not discrete group partitions.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </task> -->

    <!-- Part 2: Probability of None -->
    <task xml:id="task-cookie-prob-none-v2">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the probability that you get none of the brussels-sprout-with-turnip-chip cookies. Enter your answer as a decimal rounded to three decimal places.
            </p>
        <p>
       Probability = <fillin width="8" answer="0.215"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
  
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! <me>P(X = 0) = \frac{\binom{8}{0} \times \binom{13}{3}}{\binom{21}{3}} =\frac{286}{1330}  \approx 0.215</me>
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
</evaluate>
</evaluation>



    </task>

    <!-- Part 3: Probability of At Most One -->
    <task xml:id="task-cookie-prob-at-most-one-v2">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Compute the probability that your sample contains at most one brussels-sprout-with-turnip-chip cookie. Enter your answer as a decimal rounded to three decimal places.
            </p>
               <p>
       Probability = <fillin width="8" answer="0.684"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
  
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! <p>
                At most one means we calculate the combined probabilities for <m>X = 0</m> and <m>X = 1</m>. You found <m>P(X = 0)</m> in part (a). 
            </p>
            <p>
                Now, calculate the probability of choosing exactly 1 brussels-sprout cookie and 2 chocolate-chip cookies:
                <me>P(X = 1) = \frac{\binom{8}{1} \times \binom{13}{2}}{\binom{21}{3}} = \frac{8 \times 78}{1330} = \frac{624}{1330} \approx 0.469</me>
            </p>
            <p> Then,
                <me>P(X \le 1) = P(X = 0) + P(X = 1)
                0.215 + 0.469 = 0.684</me>
                
            </p>
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
</evaluate>
</evaluation>

    </task>

    <!-- Part 4: Expected Value & Interpretation (5-Option Multiple Choice) -->
    <task xml:id="task-cookie-expected-value">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Compute the expected number of brussels-sprout-with-turnip-chip cookies in your sample. Enter your answer as a decimal rounded to three decimal places.
            </p>
 <p>
       <m>E(X)</m> = <fillin width="8" answer="1.143"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
  
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! For a hypergeometric distribution, the expected value formula is <m>E(X) = n \left(\frac{A}{A+B}\right)</m>, where <m>n=3</m> is the sample size, <m>A=8</m> is the number of brussels-sprout cookies, and <m>B=13</m> is the number of chocolate-chip cookies. This yields <m>3 \times \left(\frac{8}{21}\right) \approx 1.143</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
</evaluate>
</evaluation>
</task>           
        </exercise>      




<exercise label="ex-hypergeometric-aerospace-assembly" xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org">
    <title>Satellite Assembly </title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            An aerospace cleanroom facility assembles tracking  modules onto satellite chassis blocks. A plug-and-play module with properly drilled alignment holes takes exactly 1 minute to secure. However, if the mounting holes were improperly drilled at the supplier facility, the technicians must manually re-drill the bracket, stretching the installation time out to 10 minutes.
        </p>
        <p>
            The staging inventory area holds a total of 20 modules in stock, of which exactly 2 contain improperly drilled holes. A technician randomly selects a sample of 5 tracking modules from the 20 available to install onto the next 5 satellites on the floor. 
        </p>

    </introduction>

    <!-- Part A: Probability all 5 fit perfectly -->
    <task xml:id="task-hyper-perfect-fit-probability">
        <statement>
            <p>
                 Find the probability that all 5 randomly selected tracking modules fit properly on the first attempt, requiring zero re-drilling work. Enter answer as a decimal rounded to three decimal places.
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>P(X = 0)</m> = <fillin answer="0.553" width="8"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate>
                <test correct="yes">
                    <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                    <feedback>
                    <p>
                        Correct! <me>P(X = 0) = \frac{\binom{2}{0}\binom{18}{5}}{\binom{20}{5}} = \frac{8568}{15504} \approx 0.553</me>
                        </p>
                    </feedback>
                </test>
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
      <!--  <solution>
            <p>
                To evaluate <m>P(X = 0)</m>, use the standard Hypergeometric probability mass function (PMF). We need to choose exactly 0 defective modules from the 2 available, and exactly 5 flawless modules from the 18 working items, divided by the total combinations of drawing 5 cards from a pool of 20:
                <me>P(X = k) = \frac{\binom{M}{k}\binom{N-M}{n-k}}{\binom{N}{n}}</me>
                <me>P(X = 0) = \frac{\binom{2}{0}\binom{18}{5}}{\binom{20}{5}} = \frac{1 \cdot 8568}{15504} = \frac{8568}{15504} \approx 0.55263</me>
                Rounded to three decimal places, the probability that all 5 modules install cleanly with zero re-drilling is exactly <strong>0.553</strong> (a 55.3% chance).
            </p>
        </solution>-->
    </task>

    <!-- Part B: Mean Time to Install -->
    <task xml:id="task-hyper-mean-time">
        <statement>
            <p>
                 Let the  random variable <m>T</m> represent the total combined time (in minutes) required to complete the installation of all 5 selected tracking modules. Find the expected installation time.
            </p>
            <p>
                Expected Mean Time = <fillin  answer="9.500" width="8"/> minutes
                
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate>
                <test correct="yes">
                    <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                    <feedback>
                    <p> Correct! <me>E(X) = 5 \cdot \left(\frac{2}{20}\right) = 5 \cdot 0.10 = 0.50 \text{ expected defective modules}</me></p>

                        <p>
                Next, define the linear time function, <m>T</m>. Each of the <m>X</m> defective units takes 10 minutes, while each of the remaining <m>(5 - X)</m> working units takes 1 minute:
                <me>T = 10X + 1(5 - X) = 9X + 5</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                By the linear properties of expected value, 
                <me>E(T) = E(9X + 5) = 9E(X) + 5 = 9(0.50) + 5 = 4.50 + 5 = 9.50</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                The expected time to finish the entire assembly sequence is 9.500 minutes.
            </p>
                    </feedback>
                </test>
            </evaluate>
             
        </evaluation>
       <!-- <solution>
            <p>
                First, calculate the expected number of defective units <m>E(X)</m> pulled inside a standard Hypergeometric draft:
                <me>E(X) = n \cdot \left(\frac{M}{N}\right) = 5 \cdot \left(\frac{2}{20}\right) = 5 \cdot 0.10 = 0.50 \text{ expected defective modules}</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                Next, define the linear time function, <m>T</m>. Each of the <m>X</m> defective units takes 10 minutes, while each of the remaining <m>(5 - X)</m> working units takes 1 minute:
                <me>T = 10X + 1(5 - X) = 9X + 5</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                By the linear properties of expected value models, apply the expectation transformation:
                <me>E(T) = E(9X + 5) = 9E(X) + 5 = 9(0.50) + 5 = 4.50 + 5 = 9.50</me>
                The expected baseline time to finish the entire assembly sequence is exactly <strong>9.500</strong> minutes.
            </p>
       
   
            <p>
                To evaluate the spread parameter of our timing distribution, we first find the variance of our underlying Hypergeometric counting variable <m>\text{Var}(X)</m>, which incorporates the finite population correction fraction:
                <me>\text{Var}(X) = n \cdot \left(\frac{M}{N}\right) \cdot \left(1 - \frac{M}{N}\right) \cdot \left(\frac{N-n}{N-1}\right)</me>
                <me>\text{Var}(X) = 5 \cdot (0.10) \cdot (0.90) \cdot \left(\frac{20-5}{20-1}\right) = 0.45 \cdot \left(\frac{15}{19}\right) = \frac{6.75}{19} \approx 0.355263</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                Now, scale this variance parameter up to match our linear time transformation matrix <m>T = 9X + 5</m>. Remember that additive constant displacements disappear under variance operations, while coefficient weights grow quadratically:
                <me>\text{Var}(T) = \text{Var}(9X + 5) = 9^2 \cdot \text{Var}(X) = 81 \cdot 0.355263 \approx 28.776315</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                Take the square root of the system variance to isolate the final standard deviation performance metric:
                <me>\sigma_T = \sqrt{\text{Var}(T)} = \sqrt{28.776315} \approx 5.36435</me>
                Let us re-verify using exact fractions to confirm precision limits:
                <me>\sigma_T = \sqrt{81 \cdot \frac{27}{76}} = \sqrt{\frac{2187}{76}} = \sqrt{28.7763} \approx 5.36435</me>
                Rounded to three decimal places, the standard deviation for the total satellite component assembly operation is exactly <strong>5.364</strong> minutes.
            </p>
        </solution>-->
    </task>


<task xml:id="task-hyper-sd-time">
        <statement>
            <p>
                 Let the  random variable <m>T</m> represent the total combined time (in minutes) required to complete the installation of all 5 selected tracking modules. Find the standard deviation for the expected installation time.
            </p>
            </statement>

            <choices randomize="yes">

             <choice correct="yes">
             <statement><p><m>5.364</m> minutes</p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! First, determine the variance of X:
                    <me>V(X) = n \cdot \left(\frac{A}{A+B}\right) \cdot \left(\frac{B}{A+B}\right) \cdot \left(\frac{A+B-n}{A+B-1}\right) = 
                    5 \cdot \left(\frac{2}{20}\right) \cdot \left(\frac{18}{20}\right) \cdot \left(\frac{15}{19}\right)\approx 0.355263</me>
                </p> 
 <p>
                Next, define the linear time function, <m>T</m>. Each of the <m>X</m> defective units takes 10 minutes, while each of the remaining <m>(5 - X)</m> working units takes 1 minute:
                <me>T = 10X + 1(5 - X) = 9X + 5</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                Then, by our variance properties, we have
                <me>V(T) = V(9X + 5) = 9^2V(X) =81(0.355263) \approx 28.776</me>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Last, take the square root to get the standard deviation of 5.364 minutes.
            </p>
                </feedback></choice>
            
            <choice> 
            <statement><p><m>28.776</m> minutes</p></statement>
              <feedback>
              <p> Incorrect. This is the variance for <m>T</m>, rather than the standard deviation.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>

            <choice> 
            <statement><p><m>0.596</m> minutes</p></statement>
              <feedback>
              <p> Incorrect. This is the standard deviation for <m>X</m>, rather than for <m>T</m>.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>

             <choice> 
            <statement><p><m>0.355</m> minutes</p></statement>
              <feedback>
              <p> Incorrect. This is the variance for <m>X</m>, rather than the standard deviation for <m>T</m>.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>

            <choice> 
            <statement><p><m>1.788</m> minutes</p></statement>
              <feedback>
              <p> Incorrect. Most likely, the variance property was applied incorrectly.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>

            <choice> 
            <statement><p><m>3.197</m> minutes</p></statement>
              <feedback>
              <p> Incorrect. This is the variance for <m>T</m>, rather than the standard deviation. Also, the variance property was applied incorrectly.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>

            </choices>
            </task>


        <!-- Part D: Time Threshold Inequality Probability (Short Answer Entry) -->
    <task xml:id="task-hyper-time-threshold-probability">
        <statement>
            
            <p>
                A project manager flags any assembly block sequence that takes strictly longer than 10 minutes to complete, as it threatens to bottleneck the downstream cleanroom satellite assembly schedule.
            </p>
            <p>
                Calculate the  probability that the installation of the 5 chosen tracking modules takes longer than 10 minutes.
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>P(T \gt 10)</m> = <fillin answer="0.447" width="8"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate>
                <test correct="yes">
                    <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                    <feedback><p>
                    Correct! If any of the tracking modules have improperly drilled holes, then installation will take more than 10 minutes. So, <m>P(T \gt 10) = P(X>0)=1-P(X=0)</m>, and we found <m>P(X=0)=0.553</m> in part (a).</p></feedback>
                </test>
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
       <!-- <solution>
            <p>
                To evaluate the schedule risk parameter, we translate the timing constraint inequality condition <m>T \gt 10</m> back into an equivalent condition for our underlying discrete counting variable <m>X</m> (the number of improperly drilled modules selected).
            </p>
            <p>
                Using the linear time transformation formula derived in Part B, <m>T = 9X + 5</m>:
                <me>T \gt 10 \implies 9X + 5 \gt 10 \implies 9X \gt 5 \implies X \gt \frac{5}{9} \approx 0.556</me>
                Because <m>X</m> is a discrete counting variable that can only take on whole integer values (<m>X \in \{0, 1, 2\}</m>), the condition <m>X \gt 0.556</m> is mathematically equivalent to stating that <m>X \ge 1</m>. 
            </p>
            <p>
                We can compute <m>P(X \ge 1)</m> efficiently by applying the **Complement Rule**:
                <me>P(T \gt 10) = P(X \ge 1) = 1 - P(X = 0)</me>
                Leveraging the baseline clean-fit probability calculated in Part A (<m>P(X = 0) = \frac{8568}{15504} \approx 0.55263</m>):
                <me>P(X \ge 1) = 1 - \frac{8568}{15504} = \frac{6936}{15504} \approx 0.44737</me>
                Rounded to three decimal places, the final probability that the assembly process takes strictly longer than 10 minutes is exactly <strong>0.447</strong> (or a 44.7% chance).
            </p>
        </solution>-->
    </task>

</exercise>



<exercise label="ex-hypergeometric-student-rentals" xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org">
    <title>Student Equipment Rentals</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            At the beginning of the semester, a campus student housing agency prepares a batch of used dorm mini-fridges that were returned by departing students, cleaned, and processed to go back out on lease agreements. Because major mechanical overhauls are not performed, a portion of the inventory is prone to malfunctioning. 
        </p>
        <p>
            Among a specific cluster of eight mini-fridges available today, exactly three are malfunctioning. A student group arrives and needs to lease four mini-fridges. So, four of the eight available  are randomly selected and leased to the students.
        </p>
        
    </introduction>

    <!-- Part A: Probability of at least one malfunctioning -->
    <task xml:id="task-rental-at-least-one">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the probability that the students receive exactly one malfunctioning mini-fridge. Enter your answer as a decimal rounded to three decimal places.
            </p>
            <p>
                Probability = <fillin mode="string" answer="0.429" width="10"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate>
                <test correct="yes">
                    <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                    <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! We track a total population of <m>N =A+B= 8</m> fridges, containing <m>A = 3</m> defective units. A sample size of <m>n = 4</m> is drawn without replacement.  
                    <me>P(X = 1)=\frac{\binom{3}{1}\binom{5}{3}}{\binom{8}{4}} \approx 0.429 </me>
                      </p>
                    </feedback>


                </test>
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
       <!-- <solution>
            <p>
                We track a total population of <m>N = 8</m> fridges, containing <m>M = 3</m> defective units and <m>N - M = 5</m> flawless units. A sample size of <m>n = 4</m> is drawn without replacement. To find <m>P(X \ge 1)</m>, it is most efficient to apply the **Complement Rule**:
                <me>P(X \ge 1) = 1 - P(X = 0)</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                Evaluate the probability of drawing absolutely zero malfunctioning appliances using the Hypergeometric probability mass function formula, <m>P(X = k) = \frac{\binom{M}{k}\binom{N-M}{n-k}}{\binom{N}{n}}</m>:
                <me>P(X = 0) = \frac{\binom{3}{0}\binom{5}{4}}{\binom{8}{4}} = \frac{1 \cdot 5}{70} = \frac{5}{70} = \frac{1}{14} \approx 0.0714</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                Completing the subtraction from certainty:
                <me>P(X \ge 1) = 1 - \frac{1}{14} = \frac{13}{14} \approx 0.92857</me>
                Rounded to four decimal places, the probability is exactly <strong>0.9286</strong>.
            </p>
        </solution>-->
    </task>

    <!-- Part B: Probability of exactly two malfunctioning -->
    <task xml:id="task-rental-exactly-two">
        <statement>
            <p>
               Find the probability that the students receive exactly two malfunctioning mini fridges. Enter your answer as a decimal rounded to three decimal places.
            </p>
            <p>
                Probability = <fillin  answer="0.429" width="10"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate>
                <test correct="yes">
                    <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                             <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! 
                    <me>P(X = 2)=\frac{\binom{3}{2}\binom{5}{2}}{\binom{8}{4}}\approx 0.429</me>
                      </p>
                    </feedback>
                </test>
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
        <!--<solution>
            <p>
                To evaluate the probability of hitting exactly two malfunctions, we configure the Hypergeometric PMF at <m>k = 2</m>:
                <me>P(X = 2) = \frac{\binom{3}{2}\binom{5}{2}}{\binom{8}{4}} = \frac{3 \cdot 10}{70} = \frac{30}{70} = \frac{3}{7} \approx 0.42857</me>
                Rounded to four decimal places, the probability is exactly <strong>0.4286</strong>.
            </p>
        </solution>-->
    </task>

    <!-- Part C: Expected Value -->
    <task xml:id="task-rental-expected-value">
        <statement>
            <p>
                 Calculate the expected number of malfunctioning appliances the group of students will get. Enter your answer as a decimal rounded to two decimal places.
            </p>
            <p>
                Expected Value = <fillin answer="1.50" width="8"/> malfunctioning fridges
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate>
                <test correct="yes">
                    <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>

                     <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! 
                    <me>E(X) =4 \times \frac{3}{8}=1.50</me>
                      </p>
                    </feedback>

                </test>
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
      <!--  <solution>
            <p>
                For a Hypergeometric variable, the long-run expectation scales simply as the sample size multiplied by the underlying defective ratio of the population pool:
                <me>E(X) = n \cdot \left(\frac{M}{N}\right) = 4 \cdot \left(\frac{3}{8}\right) = \frac{12}{8} = 1.50</me>
                The expected average is exactly <strong>1.50</strong> malfunctioning appliances.
            </p>
        </solution>-->
    </task>

    <!-- Part D: Standard Deviation -->
    <task xml:id="task-rental-standard-deviation">
        <statement>
            <p>
                 Compute the standard deviation for the expected number of malfunctioning appliances. Enter your answer as a decimal rounded to two decimal places.
            </p>
            <p>
                Standard Deviation = <fillin  answer="0.73" width="8"/> malfunctioning fridges
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate>
                <test correct="yes">
                    <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                     <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! Compute variance, and then take the square root.
                    <me>\sqrt{V(X)} = \sqrt{4 \left( \frac{3}{8}\right) \left( \frac{5}{8}\right) \left( \frac{4}{7}\right)} \approx 0.7319</me>
                      </p>
                    </feedback>
                </test>
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
       <!-- <solution>
            <p>
                To calculate the standard deviation, we evaluate the Hypergeometric variance model, which incorporates the finite population correction adjustment fraction, <m>\frac{N-n}{N-1}</m>:
                <me>\text{Var}(X) = n \cdot \left(\frac{M}{N}\right) \cdot \left(1 - \frac{M}{N}\right) \cdot \left(\frac{N-n}{N-1}\right)</me>
                <me>\text{Var}(X) = 4 \cdot \left(\frac{3}{8}\right) \cdot \left(\frac{5}{8}\right) \cdot \left(\frac{8-4}{8-1}\right) = 1.5 \cdot 0.625 \cdot \left(\frac{4}{7}\right) = \frac{15}{28} \approx 0.535714</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                Take the square root of the variance to isolate the final standard deviation performance metric:
                <me>\sigma_X = \sqrt{\frac{15}{28}} \approx 0.73192</me>
                Rounded to two decimal places, the standard deviation is exactly <strong>0.73</strong>.
            </p>
        </solution>-->
    </task>
</exercise>


