<exercises xml:id="hw-hyper-rv-turn-in" >
   <title>Written Exercises</title>
     <p>
        Recall that the binomial distribution is used when we want to calculate the likelihood that an event happens a certain number of times during <m>n</m> independent Bernoulli trials. The hypergeometric model handles dependent trials (e.g., sampling without replacement). 
     </p>
     <p>
        However, the binomial distribution can be used to <em>approximate</em> the hypergeometric distribution in certain circumstances. To better understand this approximation, answer the questions that follow.
    </p>

    <exercise label="ex-fish-hypergeometric">
        <title>Capture-Recapture Scenario</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Suppose there are 1,000 fish in a lake. During the first week, 65 fish are captured, tagged, and released. Then, 30 fish are captured two weeks later. Let <m>X</m> be a hypergeometric random variable representing the number of tagged fish found in the sample of size 30.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <!-- Interactive short answer for calculation -->
        <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
             What is the probability that 3 of the 30 fish will be tagged? In other words, use the hypergeometric probability distribution to find <m>P(X=3)</m>.  
              
            </p>
         <!--    <p>
        <fillin width="8" answer="0.184"/>
      </p> -->
    </statement>
  
