    <!-- Non-interactive conceptual explanations using essay text boxes -->
   <task>
     <statement >
    <p>
      Referring to part (b), explain why these trials can be considered Bernoulli trials but not <em>independent and identically distributed</em> Bernoulli trials (which we need in order to use the Binomial distribution). 
    </p>
  </statement>
    </task>
        
       <!-- <solution>
            <p>
                It is not binomial because true binomial distribution requires completely independent trials with a constant probability of success. To make it a true binomial setup, you would have to sample <strong>with replacement</strong>: throw each fish back into the lake immediately after checking it before drawing the next one.
            </p>
        </solution>-->
 

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Because the sample size is small relative to the size of the population (30 vs 1,000), the probabilities you calculated in part (b) are quite similar!
            </p>
            <p>
                Is it therefore reasonable to just fix the probability of success at <m>p = \frac{65}{1000} = 0.065</m> for all 30 trials (and pretend they are independent)?

                If so, this would allow us to use the binomial distribution!
            </p>
            <p>
                Determine whether the binomial distribution can provide an accurate approximation for the probability in part (a). In other words, re-compute the probability that exactly 3 of the 30 fish will be tagged using the binomial distribution with <m>p = \frac{65}{1000} = 0.065</m>. Then, compare your answer to your initial answer using the hypergeometric distribution.
            </p>

            
        <!--  <p>
           <fillin width="8" answer="0.182"/>
      </p>-->
    </statement>
