                <me>P(X=3) = \binom{30}{3}(0.065)^3(0.935)^{27} \approx 0.182</me>
                Comparing this to the hypergeometric result (<m>0.184</m>), the binomial approximation is incredibly close and accurate to within two-thousandths.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        </evaluate>
        </evaluation> -->
        </task>

        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              In general, if the sample size is at most 5% of the population, then we can use the binomial distribution to approximate the hypergeometric.
            </p>
            <p>
              Is the sample size (<m>n=30</m>)for the capture-recapture scenario less than 5% of the population? Justify your answer.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        </exercise>




<exercise label="ex-cookie-analysis">
        <title>The Cookie Jar Problem [Revisited]</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                <p>
            A cookie jar currently holds <m>21</m> total cookies: <m>13</m> are chocolate-chip cookies, and <m>8</m> are brussels-sprout-with-turnip-chip cookies. You pick <m>3</m> cookies at random from the jar without looking. Let the random variable <m>X</m> represent the number of brussels-sprout-with-turnip-chip cookies found in your sample.
        </p>
            </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
              <statement>
                <p>
